Example configurations for the modules bundled in this caddy package
====================================================================

Files are in /usr/local/share/examples/caddy-custom/. Each has a header with
links to the upstream documentation of its module.

Drop-in directory
-----------------

/usr/local/etc/caddy/Caddyfile (copy it from Caddyfile.sample) starts with

    import /usr/local/etc/caddy/Caddyfile.d/*.caddyfile

so any file named *.caddyfile in /usr/local/etc/caddy/Caddyfile.d/ is part of the
configuration (files are read in alphabetical order). Check and apply with

    caddy validate --config /usr/local/etc/caddy/Caddyfile
    service caddy reload

Copy an example and give it the .caddyfile extension:

    cp /usr/local/share/examples/caddy-custom/ratelimit.Caddyfile \
       /usr/local/etc/caddy/Caddyfile.d/ratelimit.caddyfile

Rules that decide what can live in a drop-in:

 * Site blocks (example.com { ... }) and snippets can be in any number of
   drop-ins.
 * The global options block ({ ... } without an address) may exist only ONCE
   in the whole configuration and must come before every site block. Either
   keep it in /usr/local/etc/caddy/Caddyfile above the import line, or put it in a
   single drop-in that sorts first, e.g. 00-global.caddyfile. A second global
   block, or a global block after a site block, is an error ("server block
   without any key is global configuration, and if used, it must be first").
 * The sample Caddyfile also contains a "localhost" demo site. Remove
   or change it if it conflicts with your sites.

What each example contains
--------------------------

 file                      global options block        site blocks   as drop-in
 ------------------------  --------------------------  ------------  ----------------------
 wstunnel.Caddyfile        no                          yes           yes, as is
 ratelimit.Caddyfile       no                          yes           yes, as is
 wstunnel-rules.yaml       not a Caddyfile             -             referenced by
                                                                     restrict_config
 dynamicdns.Caddyfile      yes (dynamic_dns)           yes           global part: only
                                                                     00-global.caddyfile
 cloudflare.Caddyfile      yes (acme_dns)              yes           global part: only
                                                                     00-global.caddyfile;
                                                                     or use the per-site
                                                                     "tls { dns ... }" form,
                                                                     then it is a plain
                                                                     drop-in
 layer4.Caddyfile          yes (https_port, layer4)    yes           global part: only
                                                                     00-global.caddyfile
 layer4-alt-port.Caddyfile yes (layer4)                yes           global part: only
                                                                     00-global.caddyfile

If several examples have a global block (for example dynamicdns and layer4),
merge their contents into ONE global block, for instance:

    # /usr/local/etc/caddy/Caddyfile.d/00-global.caddyfile
    {
        dynamic_dns {
            ...
        }
        layer4 {
            ...
        }
    }

and put the site blocks of the examples into separate drop-ins.

Secrets (API tokens) are read from /usr/local/etc/caddy/environment (sh syntax,
sourced by the rc script, see the "environment" example in this directory).
Do not put secrets in rc.conf, it is world readable.
